---
title: CSS Isolator
description: Scope CSS variables and CSS rules to a specific container instance using auto-generated IDs.
format: hd-doc v1
date: 2026-06-17
tags: [css-isolator, styling, scope, css-variables]
---

<h1>CSS Isolator</h1>

<p>Webflow's Native Component and Code Component styling doesn't directly support styling individual component instances. CSS Isolator fills that gap — wrap any group of components or elements in a CSS Isolator and provide CSS or CSS variable overrides that apply only to that specific instance.</p>

<p><strong>Group:</strong> Style</p>

<h2>Features</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Provide custom CSS that applies only to the slotted content — works with Native Components, Code Components, and Shared Libraries</li>
  <li>Provide CSS variable overrides that apply only to the slotted content — works with Code Components, Native Components, and Shared Libraries</li>
  <li>Each isolator gets a unique auto-generated ID so styles never leak to other instances</li>
</ul>

<h2>Properties</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>cssVars</code></td><td>Text</td><td>—</td><td>CSS custom property overrides applied to the scoped container (e.g., <code>--color-brand: #ff6600;</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>css</code></td><td>Text</td><td>—</td><td>Full CSS rules to scope to this container. Selectors are automatically prefixed with the container's unique ID.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>slot</code></td><td>Slot</td><td>—</td><td>Components and elements that should be affected by the isolated CSS rules.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>How It Works</h2>

<p>When the component mounts, it generates a unique ID (UUID v4) and creates a wrapping <code>&lt;div id="{uuid}"&gt;</code>. Any CSS you provide in the <code>css</code> property is transformed so that every selector is prefixed with <code>#{uuid}</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-css">/* Your input */
.button { color: red; }
h2 { font-size: 2rem; }

/* Applied to the page */
#a1b2c3d4 .button { color: red; }
#a1b2c3d4 h2 { font-size: 2rem; }</code></pre>

<p>CSS custom properties from <code>cssVars</code> are applied directly to the container element, making them available to all descendants via the CSS cascade.</p>

<h2>CSS Variables</h2>

<p>Use the <code>cssVars</code> property to override design tokens or CSS custom properties for everything inside the isolator:</p>

<pre><code class="language-css">--color-brand: #ff6600;
--font-size-heading: 2.5rem;
--spacing-section: 4rem;</code></pre>

<p>These variables cascade normally within the scoped container. Components that consume these variables will pick up the overridden values.</p>

<h2>Scoped CSS Rules</h2>

<p>Use the <code>css</code> property for full CSS rules. Selectors are prefixed automatically — you write standard CSS:</p>

<pre><code class="language-css">.card {
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--color-brand);
}</code></pre>

<h2>Use Cases</h2>

<h3>Per-section theme overrides</h3>

<p>Apply different brand colors to two sections of the same page that use the same components:</p>

<pre><code class="language-css">/* Section 1 CSS Isolator cssVars */
--color-brand: #0055ff;
--color-accent: #00ccff;

/* Section 2 CSS Isolator cssVars */
--color-brand: #ff3300;
--color-accent: #ffaa00;</code></pre>

<h3>One-off component styling</h3>

<p>Style a specific component instance without affecting global styles or creating new Webflow classes:</p>

<pre><code class="language-css">.my-component { padding: 2rem; background: #f5f5f5; }</code></pre>

<h2>Limitations</h2>

<ul>
  <li>In some cases, styling may not render correctly at design-time due to differences in the Webflow design canvas. Most styles will be visible in the Designer, but publish to staging or use Preview mode to verify complex cases.</li>
  <li>The selector prefixer uses a regex-based approach, not a full CSS parser. Complex selectors involving pseudo-elements (<code>::before</code>, <code>::after</code>) or at-rules (<code>@media</code>, <code>@keyframes</code>) may not be prefixed correctly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>HTML Encoding</h2>

<p>Both <code>cssVars</code> and <code>css</code> values are HTML-decoded before being applied, so HTML entities like <code>&amp;gt;</code> and <code>&amp;amp;</code> entered through the Webflow Designer text fields are correctly handled.</p>
